        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "26bde4a4-e1ca-5778-ae0f-4fa44e3f19d1",
        ":question/problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=4.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=4,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2=4.$",
